 one of the \"principal architects\" of what became known as the Nashville Sound or Countrypolitan style of country music. \"Atkins and Kramer are two of the principal architects of what became known as the Nashville Sound, or Country Politan.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 38: \"Heartbreak Hotel\" by Elvis Presley","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ES35PYeAwfs"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Minimal Production Input","body":"According to Scotty Moore, Chet Atkins' only production suggestion during the \"Heartbreak Hotel\" session was to \"just keep doing what they'd been doing.\" \"According to Scotty Moore, his only suggestion was that they just keep doing what they'd been doing.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 38: \"Heartbreak Hotel\" by Elvis Presley","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ES35PYeAwfs"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Presence at \"Heartbreak Hotel\" Session","body":"Though Steve Sholes was the credited producer, Chet Atkins was nominally in charge of running the \"Heartbreak Hotel\" session for Elvis Presley. He played an acoustic rhythm guitar part, which was considered an underutilization of his talent as \"probably the greatest country guitarist of his generation.\" \"But by all accounts Atkins was nominally in charge of actually running the session, and certainly there would be no other reason for having Atkins there.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 38: \"Heartbreak Hotel\" by Elvis Presley","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ES35PYeAwfs"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Producer at RCA","body":"Chet Atkins was a legendary country music producer at RCA. He was instrumental in offering Charlie Pride a recording contract in 1965 after hearing his demo and being impressed by his voice. \"Chet Atkins, the legendary country music producer at RCA.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 53 - More than Charley Pride - African Americans in 70s Country","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 53 - More than Charley Pride - African Americans in 70s Country","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Producer for Janice Martin","body":"Chet Atkins produced Janice Martin's recording sessions and also played on most of her early recordings, contributing to a sound similar to Elvis Presley's records. \"Chet Atkins, who also produced Janice's sessions, and Floyd Kramer were both on most of Janice's early recordings.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 40: \"Drugstore Rock 'n' Roll\" by Janis Martin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l0i2p9TelN0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Regret Over Orbison's RCA Recordings","body":"Chet Atkins, who played guitar on Roy Orbison's RCA recordings, later expressed regret for his approach, stating the records were \"typical Nashville at that time\" and failed to be innovative. He blamed himself for not recognizing Orbison's greatness and vocal quality. \"Chet Atkins, like Sam Phillips, just didn't get Roy Orbison. He would later say, we did some pretty good records, but they were typical Nashville at that time, and we didn't reach out and try to do something different. I blame myself for that. I should have seen the greatness in him and the quality of his voice.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 42: \"Ooby Dooby\" by Roy Orbison and the Teen Kings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1zvzpXqr-k8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Satisfaction with Makeshift Echo","body":"Chet Atkins was satisfied with the makeshift echo chamber created for the \"Heartbreak Hotel\" session, which involved setting up a speaker and feeding in sound from microphones at the other end of the room. \"creating a makeshift echo chamber which satisfied Chet Atkins.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 38: \"Heartbreak Hotel\" by Elvis Presley","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ES35PYeAwfs"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":237475213543847,"type":"album","name":"Chet Atkins' Gallopin' Guitar","list_id":194769329123789,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Country Boogie","src":"musthear","rank":2,"year":1952,"note":null,"yt":"kOAd6jOoiSA","scaruffi":null},{"id":237475213543847,"type":"album","name":"Chet Atkins' Gallopin' Guitar","list_id":37273800199734,"list":"RYM Top — Pop — Standards","src":"musthear","rank":186,"year":1952,"note":null,"yt":"kOAd6jOoiSA","scaruffi":null},{"id":237095895539985,"type":"album","name":"A Session With Chet Atkins","list_id":194769329123789,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Country Boogie","src":"musthear","rank":5,"year":1954,"note":null,"yt":"iVSXkH6aK_U","scaruffi":null},{"id":4522851754435,"type":"album","name":"Chet Atkins in 3 Dimensions","list_id":194769329123789,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Country Boogie","src":"musthear","rank":4,"year":1956,"note":null,"yt":"0LqTk8VqZdo","scaruffi":null},{"id":150403011622738,"type":"album","name":"Stringin' Along With Chet Atkins","list_id":194769329123789,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Country Boogie","src":"musthear","rank":8,"year":1956,"note":null,"yt":"hi3f9moF_K0","scaruffi":null},{"id":172457679495629,"type":"album","name":"Finger-Style Guitar","list_id":222913818870117,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Nashville Sound","src":"musthear","rank":46,"year":1956,"note":null,"yt":"Hm-mROh4CGM","scaruffi":null},{"id":4522851754435,"type":"album","name":"Chet Atkins in 3 Dimensions","list_id":222913818870117,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Nashville Sound","src":"musthear","rank":136,"year":1956,"note":null,"yt":"0LqTk8VqZdo","scaruffi":null},{"id":172457679495629,"type":"album","name":"Fi